WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.806 --> 00:00:03.550
I have a report
now, that they're

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.990
sending a new rover to Mars.

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:10.140
And we are responsible for doing
one thing, which is programming

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.650
the algorithm inside
the rover, which

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.130
checks if numbers are prime.

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:18.620
Because let's say our rover
is communicating using RSA,

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:23.270
it needs an algorithm inside
that can do a primality test.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:27.660
Now, when you design a rover,
or anything to go into space,

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.830
you have to be very
efficient in every way.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.980
So the components used
have to be very light,

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:35.750
the amount of power
every subsystem

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.570
it uses has to be minimal.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:42.520
You have to be saving
energy and mass

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.370
at every point in
the design process.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:47.990
So we have our work
cut out for us,

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:50.910
because we have to be able
to deal with numbers up

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:57.160
to this size, and it has
to do it with very fast.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.950
So, if we give it a very small
input, let's say just 90,

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:04.950
it should be able to
calculate that almost as

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.920
fast as this entire number.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.940
So as the input
grows, we don't want

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:13.460
to see any of this
noticeable slow down.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.890
Now I want to analyze the user
questions, or the ideas users

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.680
have had, which
were really good,

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.560
from a mathematical standpoint.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.700
We are checking if n
is prime or composite.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.600
So given some number n, think
of the space of all possible n's

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.830
as, say, if n is 100, then
this space is 2 to 100.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.860
And what our algorithm is doing
is it's searching this space.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:38.360
OK.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.150
So you can think of algorithms
as searching a space.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:44.950
And at each point it's
asking-- think of it as a step,

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.770
a primitive step--
it's asking a question.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:51.920
And it's actually a
composite test, the question.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.650
So say, we're at number a.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:58.020
We would say, in the
trial division method,

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.080
is n divided by a?

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.050
We just try this.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:04.180
We drop a here, and
we try if n divides a.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.840
And we see if the
remainder is 0,

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.669
which means is the divisor of n.

00:02:09.669 --> 00:02:13.660
Then we can say, ah, we know
100% we raised the flag.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.880
We're 100% sure its composite.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.400
Otherwise, at each step,
we aren't quite sure.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.420
It might be prime,
but we're not sure.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:26.840
So we continue searching
until we hit the end.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.860
And remember our wall,
in this case was,

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.180
at the square root of n.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.180
That's if the worst
case situation

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.970
occurs when n is prime.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:37.790
We search for all the way
to the square root of n.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:42.360
And then we can say, ah, it is
prime and then we're 100% sure.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.020
So in the case where n is even
a multiple of 2 prime-- so 7

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:51.820
times 7 equals 49-- if we
throw 49 into our algorithm,

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:53.320
the worst case occurs.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.490
We go all the way until
the square root of n.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:00.430
So the first set of
questions, from, for example,

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:05.570
the fourth dimension asks, once
we rule out 2 as not divisible,

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.830
then all multiple of
2 could be ruled out.

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:12.030
The same for 3, 4, 5, et cetera.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.510
So that's a really great point.

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:18.770
Our old algorithm was
stepping along one at a time.

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:20.620
But we could be
using patterns we

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:23.960
know about composite numbers.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:28.650
Such as, we know for
sure that if a number

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:30.502
is divisible by 2,
then its composite.

00:03:30.502 --> 00:03:31.460
If it's greater than 2.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:32.640
So 2 is prime.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.450
But then we can say,
oh 4 is composite.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.300
2 oh-- I missed 5 here.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.090
Sorry about that.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:44.480
4, 6, 8, 10.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:51.590
And instead, we can take this
step like this, 3, 5, 7, 9.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:55.330
So one category of
questions, they all

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.360
try to reduce this space.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:00.216
So if we eliminate
all the even numbers,

00:04:00.216 --> 00:04:01.840
now the checked space,
instead of being

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.040
up to the square root
of n, is the square root

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.410
of n divided by 2.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:09.540
And we can find other
patterns in composite numbers

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.400
to try to make
this even smaller.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.910
The other type of
question concerns the case

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:17.920
where we find a
composite witness.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.980
That is we find an a that
allows us to say, oh,

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.830
we know n is composite.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:25.930
Lola said, "Wouldn't
leaving the loop as soon

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:27.680
as we find prime
check equals false

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:29.760
cut down on some of the time?"

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.170
And yes, that's totally correct.

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:33.880
And it's something
we want to do.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:38.070
As soon as we are searching
along using some step-- defined

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.170
by whatever pattern
we're using--

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:44.930
we find a composite witness,
that means it passes our test,

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:48.550
and we say 100% composite,
we should halt early.

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:50.350
We stop and say, oh, we're done.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.460
We won't continue searching.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:57.310
And this halting is great,
except it doesn't help us

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:04.100
in the worst case,
which is, if n is prime,

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:07.390
then the early
halting won't help us.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:10.830
And now we can visualize
how these improvements allow

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.400
us to reduce the
space, thus preventing

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.340
as many checks happening inside
the computer, which, depending

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:23.480
on computer, will reduce
the amount of time it takes.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:25.320
So now I can show
you the visualization

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:28.210
I've set up below, which
allows us to compare

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:30.710
two algorithms based
on how many steps

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:32.580
occur during their execution.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.290
On the left is
algorithm a, which

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:39.080
is trial division, which checks
from 2 to the square root of n.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:42.070
And on the right is
algorithm b, which

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:44.510
is, let's just say our
improved algorithm.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:48.880
And in this case,
I've applied the check

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:50.310
if it's divisible by 2.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:52.970
So we only do half
as many steps.

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:56.830
And I also terminate
early, in this algorithm b.

00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:57.720
So it's not perfect.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.460
I've just applied a
few user modifications,

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:02.120
so we can see what happens.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:06.810
And now I'm going to just
play with this to show you it.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:10.140
Here we can see as I
scroll, we see algorithm a.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:13.120
We have the output, whether
it's composite or prime,

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:16.270
and at the bottom you'll
see the number of steps.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:19.900
So the first thing to notice
is on the right-hand side,

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:22.210
every other number
takes only one step.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:24.650
And we know why that
is, because if it's

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:28.060
an even number, such
as this, it will quit.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:31.340
So our old algorithm
took 314 steps.

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:33.847
And our new algorithm
only took one step,

00:06:33.847 --> 00:06:35.680
because it checked if
it was divisible by 2.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:39.480
So that seems like a
really nice optimization.

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:42.160
However, as we build
our input, you'll

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:46.030
notice the steps increase.

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:51.820
And the bar grows and
turns red, once we approach

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:53.510
the region where
we're going to crash.

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:55.380
So this red line up
here is step limit.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:58.340
If your bar hits
there, we fail, which

00:06:58.340 --> 00:06:59.880
means our rover would break.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:02.549
And in this case, the
browser will actually crash.

00:07:02.549 --> 00:07:03.840
So I'll try to get close to it.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.080
So I'm close to it now.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.950
And it's running very slow.

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:09.080
I can feel that
my browser is just

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:12.500
about to crash and give
me the circle of death.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:15.410
But notice that our improved
algorithm took only two steps

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:16.590
in that case.

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.715
But remember the worst case.

00:07:19.715 --> 00:07:23.600
I have a few large prime
numbers saved here, for example.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:25.575
We have to be able
to handle any case.

00:07:25.575 --> 00:07:27.700
We don't know what we're
throwing at our algorithm.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:31.770
So if I throw a large prime
at it, look what happens.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:34.550
Algorithm b, as we
know, is taking half

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:36.920
as many times steps
in the worst case,

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.880
but it's still
taking in many steps

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.300
here, because it's
the worst case, right?

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:44.770
So we're getting close
to crashing here.

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:46.790
And this is not a
very long prime.

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:49.150
We're still way under 15 digits.

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:52.640
And when I put this
12-- I think this

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:57.990
is a 12 digit prime
into our algorithm,

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:59.750
let's see what happens.

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:02.090
It's lagging.

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:03.579
Maybe it's going to crash.

00:08:03.579 --> 00:08:04.370
Look what happened.

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:07.100
Both algorithms
went way overboard.

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:08.950
So it didn't work.

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:12.920
Our improved algorithm
is not good enough yet.

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:15.120
But now that we have an
understanding of what we're

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:16.840
trying to improve,
which is number

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:19.130
of steps in the worst case.

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:21.540
And we also have
this cool tool, which

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:25.550
allows us to visualize
how fast it's growing.

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:28.700
How fast the number of steps
grows as our input grows.

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:32.470
And below, I'll explain
how I set this up.

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:35.799
So you can set up your
algorithm and compare it

00:08:35.799 --> 00:08:39.979
with the base case, and see
how much better you can do.

